    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> built two colonial empires: one in North America and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a>, lost in 1763, and a larger one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a> and Asia.</li><li>Plantation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavery">slavery</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> the world's richest colony, while the later empire extracted raw materials for French industry.</li><li>Nationalist wars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> ended it, leaving French as an official language in twenty-nine countries.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>By the 1780s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, now Haiti, produced around 40 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and some 60 per cent of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coffee-economy">coffee</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

